// The Frobenius coin problem (named after Georg Frobenius; the
// two-coin case was known to James Joseph Sylvester in 1884): for coprime
// coin denominations `a`, `b` (both greater than one), the largest amount
// that cannot be paid with nonnegative numbers of `a`-coins and `b`-coins is
//     ab - a - b.
// Every amount larger than `ab - a - b` IS payable, and `ab - a - b` itself
// is not.  The general problem — the largest non-representable value for
// `m >= 3` denominations — is open in closed form (it is the subject of the
// "Frobenius number" literature); the two-coin case is completely solved.
// The theorem is also called the "Chicken McNugget theorem" from the classic
// example with the 6-, 9- and 20-piece boxes.
//
// Proof sketch (two parts).
//
// Non-representability of `ab - a - b`: suppose `a x + b y = ab - a - b`.
// Adding `a + b` to both sides gives `a (x + 1) + b (y + 1) = ab`, so
// `a (x + 1) = b (a - (y + 1))`, and since `a` is coprime to `b`, `a`
// divides `a - (y + 1)`, forcing `x + 1 >= b` (as `a (x + 1) = b(...) >= b`
// ... more precisely `a | (y + 1) - a`-style bookkeeping); then
// `a x + b y >= a (b - 1) > ab - a - b`, a contradiction.
//
// Representability of every `n > ab - a - b`: write `n = a q + r` with
// `0 <= r < a` by division with remainder.  Since `b` is coprime to `a`, the
// residues `r, r + b, ..., r + (a - 1) b` cover all residues modulo `a`
// exactly once; choose the unique `0 <= y < a` with `r + b y ≡ 0 (mod a)`.
// Then `n - b y` is divisible by `a` and, because `n > ab - a - b`, the
// bound `n - b y >= n - b (a - 1) > a - 1 >= 0` shows `n - b y` is a
// nonnegative multiple of `a`.
//
// The library has the division and coprimality machinery
// (`number_theory/bezout.ac`, `number_theory/crt.ac`) that these two
// arguments use, but the modular-residue chase is not yet packaged; the
// statement is recorded without proof.

